    "knowledge_point_id": "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length",
    "problem": "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2}$ on $[0,3]?$",
    "worked_solution": "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{3}{2})6{x}^{2}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{1/2}={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}(2{x}^{3}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
